    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2d767210_861b8977",
        "filename": "virtualization/aidl/android/hardware/hypervisor/IHypervisor.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-17T00:20:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "it is pretty heavy to add a process and a HAL to expose something informational like this. Could we use a system property?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "87a099b86cd8a8ec8917583b002b7cb9b7f3f42e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8d71a455_8c2ae037",
        "filename": "virtualization/aidl/android/hardware/hypervisor/IHypervisor.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-17T00:46:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree with Steven. A HAL just for a version string seems to be an overkill to me. In addition, the value is expected to be immutable. Where is the source of truth? I think that this information can be provided as a bootconfig (which will then automatically translates into a system property) or a node in DT when Android is booted inside a guest VM.",
      "parentUuid": "2d767210_861b8977",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "87a099b86cd8a8ec8917583b002b7cb9b7f3f42e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a6bb2ef2_9295225c",
        "filename": "virtualization/aidl/android/hardware/hypervisor/IHypervisor.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1133963
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-17T15:59:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t disagree with the heavyweight argument and I\u0027m happy to explore options. Here\u0027s a couple thoughts on why go HAL route\n\nA system property, or DT entry, is pretty much a \"dead end\", i.e. it can represent a static text string but not any kind of dynamic behavior. Even though the HAL is pretty minimal now, I could see trying to add new features (memory sharing is one that AAOS may end up wanting for example), and having the HAL in place makes it a more natural extension.\n\nHaving a HAL in place also has the value of one standard place to do this kind of work. A system property or DT entry is fine and all, but it also feels highly vendor specific, and there is no real nudge to partners towards \"do the standard Android thing\". The HAL offers that IMO.\n\nLast point, I am not sure if system properties can be individually secured. Regular apps really have no business getting their hands on this information, and I\u0027m not sure if there\u0027s SELinux policy mechanisms that can say \"thou shalt not touch this specific property\". This may just be a gap in my knowledge, but I know how to secure a HAL, I don\u0027t know how to secure a system property.",
      "parentUuid": "8d71a455_8c2ae037",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "87a099b86cd8a8ec8917583b002b7cb9b7f3f42e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
